From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 06:16:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 033/184] segacd: follow the BIOS exchange exactly, including
 the order

Reading two routines out of the BIOS was not the same as reading the
code that calls them, and the caller is where the rest of it was.

Three corrections, one of them mine from the previous commit:

The wait is for DRS to go *low*, not high. `btst #1,$FF8037 / dbeq`
exits when the condition is true, and btst sets Z when the bit is
zero, so the BIOS spins until the incoming packet has finished
arriving. At a glance it reads as the opposite, and I read it at a
glance.

The acknowledgement comes before the command is loaded, not after.
The BIOS reads the status, writes #4 to the control register, and only
then fills the command registers.

And it writes a command on every exchange -- 75 times a second, a NOP
when it has nothing to ask. This driver wrote one about twice a second
and left seventy-odd exchanges untouched, which is not how a link
clocked by both ends behaves.

The checksum convention is confirmed against the same code: the BIOS
sums all ten status bytes, inverts, masks to a nibble and requires
zero, which is the same test written the other way round.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 68 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------------------
 1 file changed, 42 insertions(+), 26 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 3f2aee2..96dbfb0 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -189,14 +189,13 @@ void segacd_cdd_init(void)
  * the console's BIOS ROM, but the boot process decompresses it into
  * PRG-RAM $0000-$5FFF -- exactly the region the audit payload already
  * snapshots -- so Sega's own CDD routine can simply be read. It does
- * three things per exchange, and we were doing one of them:
+ * four things per exchange, and we were doing one of them:
  *
- *     btst  #1,$FF8037     wait for DRS: the status packet is ready
- *     dbeq  d0,...         (bounded, and it gives up)
- *     lea   $FF8038,a1     read ten status bytes
- *     move.l (a1)+,(a0)+   as long, long, word
- *     lea   $FF8042,a1     write ten command bytes, same shape
- *     move.b #4,$FF8037    <- hand it over and re-arm the link
+ *     btst  #1,$FF8037     spin until DRS goes low: the incoming
+ *     dbeq  d0,...         packet has finished arriving
+ *     lea   $FF8038,a1     read ten status bytes, long/long/word
+ *     move.b #4,$FF8037    acknowledge: HOCK stays, DTS and DRS clear
+ *     lea   $FF8042,a1     only now load the ten command bytes
  *
  * That last write is the whole answer. Four discs went looking for a
  * trigger among writes to the command registers, and there isn't one:
@@ -207,10 +206,8 @@ void segacd_cdd_init(void)
  * reporting its own status and answering "invalid" to questions it was
  * never asked. Every symptom, exactly.
  *
- * The waiting was wrong too, and backwards: DRS set does not mean the
- * bus is busy, it means the packet has arrived. The previous driver
- * refused to send a command in precisely the moment the BIOS chooses
- * to send one. */
+ * The acknowledgement also comes *before* the command is loaded, not
+ * after it. Order matters here in a way it does not in a mailbox. */
 static void cdd_command(const UBYTE *nib)       /* nine nibbles */
 {
     volatile ULONG *l = (volatile ULONG *)0xFF8042;
@@ -352,16 +349,21 @@ void segacd_int_4_c(void)
 
     cdd_irqs++;
 
-    /* Wait for the packet rather than assuming the interrupt means it
-     * has fully landed: the gate array raises level 4 before the last
-     * nibbles are in. Bounded, because a drive that stops talking must
-     * not take the machine with it. */
+    /* Wait for DRS to go *low*. It reads as "a status packet is on its
+     * way in", so the BIOS spins until the incoming transfer is over
+     * and only then reads the registers -- bounded, and it gives up
+     * rather than hang.
+     *
+     * Getting the direction of this test wrong cost a disc: dbeq exits
+     * when its condition is true, btst sets Z when the bit is *zero*,
+     * so `btst #1,$FF8037 / dbeq` waits for the bit to clear. Read at
+     * a glance it looks like the opposite, and I read it at a glance. */
     {
         UWORD n;
         for (n = 0; n < 512; n++) {
             ctrl = *CDD_CTRL_RD;
             cdd_ctrl_ever |= (UBYTE)(ctrl & (CDD_DTS | CDD_DRS));
-            if (ctrl & CDD_DRS)
+            if (!(ctrl & CDD_DRS))
                 break;
         }
     }
@@ -369,17 +371,31 @@ void segacd_int_4_c(void)
 
     cdd_poll();
 
-    if (cdd_pending) {
-        cdd_pending = 0;
-        cdd_command(cdd_queued);
-        if (cdd_sent < 0xFF)
-            cdd_sent++;
-    }
-
-    /* Hand the exchange over: HOCK stays set, DTS and DRS go to zero.
-     * This is the step the driver was missing entirely. */
+    /* Acknowledge the exchange before sending anything: HOCK stays
+     * set, DTS and DRS go to zero. The BIOS does this the moment it
+     * has the status in hand and only then loads the next command,
+     * which is the ordering this driver had inside out. */
     *CDD_CTRL_B = CDD_HOCK;
-    cdd_ctrl_ever |= (UBYTE)(*CDD_CTRL_RD & (CDD_DTS | CDD_DRS));
+
+    /* Send something every single exchange, as the BIOS does: the
+     * queued question if there is one, otherwise a NOP. The BIOS never
+     * leaves the command registers unwritten, and a serial link that is
+     * clocked by both ends having something to say is not one to leave
+     * half-idle -- this driver was writing a command roughly twice a
+     * second and letting seventy-odd exchanges go by untouched. */
+    {
+        static const UBYTE nop[9] = { CDD_CMD_STAT, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
+
+        if (cdd_pending) {
+            cdd_pending = 0;
+            cdd_command(cdd_queued);
+            if (cdd_sent < 0xFF)
+                cdd_sent++;
+        } else {
+            cdd_command(nop);
+        }
+        cdd_ctrl_ever |= (UBYTE)(*CDD_CTRL_RD & (CDD_DTS | CDD_DRS));
+    }
 
     /* A boolean cannot tell a dead link from a flaky one. Count how
      * many of the last sixteen packets checksummed and report that:
